Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023

STRATEGIC REPORT

The Directors present their Strategic Report for Brown Shipley & Co. Limited (‘Brown Shipley’, ‘BSCO’ or the
‘Company’) for the year ended 31 December 2023.

Brown Shipley’s principal activities
Brown Shipley’s principal activity is to provide wealth management services comprising investment
management, wealth planning, lending, and other private banking services to its clients, which mainly consist
of private individuals. The Company aims to increase its client base and Assets Under Management (AUM)
through organic growth and marketing.

In line with our focus on principal activities we have continued to rationalise our non-core portfolios. During
2023 a sale of Court of Protection business was agreed and a one-off gain reflected in the Income Statement.
In January 2024, Brown Shipley signed an agreement to sell a book of Affluent clients to an external third
party. At the time of signing the accounts the sale has not been communicated to clients and it is too early to
estimate the financial impact of the sale.

The Campany’s vision is to be recognised as a trusted partner to its clients and as a leading private bank,
providing integrated wealth management solutions to those clients. The Company's mission is to preserve
and grow each client’s wealth across generations.

Managing clients’ funds on a discretionary basis is at the heart of the Company’s offering. Having full discretion
with securities held by our nominee and cash held by us as an authorised bank, allows our investment
managers maximum flexibility and enables us to provide quality services efficiently and at a reasonable cost.
Increasingly the service provided to clients is that of Wealth Management, a combination of investment
management along with ongoing wealth planning. These services enable the Company to provide clients with
advice on all aspects of their financial affairs. The Company also provides advisory management and
execution only investment management services.

Brown Shipley is an authorised bank providing a range of services to its clients. Deposit facilities and lending
are offered as a complementary service to wealth management. Loans are only offered to enhance the service
provided to existing customers or to establish new private banking relationships. Loans are typically made on
a secured basis, where the security held comprises mainly investment management portfolios and UK
residential property assets.

Business review

Brown Shipley has produced a profit before tax in 2023 despite the challenging market conditions discussed
below. Clients value the advice and guidance that we offer and given the economic, political, and market
environment they have faced through 2023, access to personal service and advice has helped us strengthen
relationships and demonstrate value to the clients we serve.

As reported last year, investors saw negative returns across all risk profiles in 2022 driven by the worst equity
performance since the global financial crisis and the deepest bond bear market in 70 years. 2023 ultimately
delivered a turnaround with stocks and bonds both performing well as inflation continued to fall. Western
economies avoided meaningful recessions, and corporate earnings remained surprisingly resilient. Towards
the end of the year, interest rate uncertainty gave way to geopolitical uncertainty, but any meaningful market
impact was short-lived.

In 2023 we navigated the volatility to deliver strong positive returns across all risk profiles. Our overweight
bond position was supported by the drop in yields (and rise in prices) towards year end and our focus on
quality stocks meant we were able to capture the rally, while mitigating some of the downside volatility.

In 2023 we reported two significant partnerships which enhance our global capability and breadth of offering.
The partnership agreement between Quintet, our Parent Company, and BlackRock, built on the principle of
co-creation enhances our capability, our reach, our access and our scale ensuring market leading solutions
for our clients. The partnership with Moonfare enables clients to have greater access to private markets, an
increasingly important asset class in both driving return and diversifying risk.

Consumer Duty is a new regulation which sets out higher standards for financial services firms to deliver good
outcomes for their clients. Its intention is to fundamentally improve how client are served. At Brown Shipley,
ocuSign Envelope ID: 20025163-16F6-447B-B2B2-C72D09F8AB57 «

Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023
STRATEGIC REPORT

everything we do is done with our clients’ best interests at heart and we see Consumer Duty as an opportunity
to continue to elevate our standards in delivering good outcomes to our clients.

We are pleased that in May 2023 we achieved a Crystal Mark approval on our website awarded by the Plain
English Campaign for being clear and concise. In March 2023, the Company won the UK Private Bank award
for Best Credit Provider at the Wealth Briefing European Awards.

Financial review of the Company and key performance indicators (KPIs)

The Company’s reported profit before tax was £1.2m (2022: £2.3m). However, there were several one-off or
non-recurring items both in the current and prior year, particularly around the accounting for the acquisition in
2019 of NW Brown & Co (‘NWB’) and sale of a small non-core book of those customer relationships in April
2022 and sale of another non-core book in November 2023.

When these are stripped out or made comparable, the figures show an adjusted operating profit before tax of
£6.3m (2022: profit £4.5m). Given the difficult trading environment experienced during the year, it is a
satisfactory outcome for the year.

