Net operating income
Total net operating income increased by 3% to £95.7m (2022: £93.0m). The growth includes one off gains of
£0.7m from sale of Court of Protection business.

Net operating income also includes additional consideration of £1.4m relating to the purchase of NWB
acquisition which is offset by a decrease in the provision for pension transfer redress of £1.9m and
corresponding decrease of £1.1m on Professional Indemnity insurance relating to the redress resulting in loss
of £0.5m on the NWB acquisition.

Fee and Commission income during the year is £56.9m (2022: £57.2m), a small reduction in comparison to
prior year.

The banking element of the business continues to grow in line with the Company's strategy. After many years
of very low interest rates, the Bank of England has been increasing the base interest rate from 3.50% in
December 2022 to 5.25% by December 2023 which has had a strong positive impact of 53% on margins in
2023 to £27.4m (2022: £18.0m), as spreads widened.

Net gains from financial instruments at fair value through profit and loss amounted to £3.3m (2022: £7.7m).
Fair value on interest rate swaps held by the Company for hedging purposes decreased by £5.4m (2022:
increase £1.9m) due to the decrease in market swap rate curves expectation. This was offset by an increase
in FX trading volumes to £4.9m (2022: £3.5m) due to volatile market conditions.

Transfer pricing income from the recharge to the parent company of staff employed by BSCO but undertaking
Quintet Private Bank (Europe) S.A. (‘Quintet’) Group roles decreased to £8.8m (2022: £9.2m). Such staff in

