Costs

In headline terms costs rose by 3.3% to £91.0m (2022: £88. 1m) largely due to a further increase of £2.1m in
the transfer pricing expense charged to Brown Shipley from Quintet to £14.5m (2022: £12.4m), to reflect the
additional services received by Brown Shipley during the year and its share of group-wide project costs.

Average staff numbers decreased by 26 during the year, with average full time equivalent (FTE) staff falling
to 362 (2022: 388). Despite the planned decrease in average staff numbers, staff costs have remained stable
during the year, £53.8m (2022: £53.7m) due to staff redundancy provisions and external wage inflationary
pressures.

The Company reduced the provision for the defined benefit pension transfer redress plus associated legal
and professional fees by £1.9m (2022: decrease of £0.8m), which was partly offset by a reduction in expected
Pl Insurance recoveries of £1.1m (2022: £0.9m). Settlement payments of £0.6m (2022: £3.3m) were made
during the year, with £0.1m (2022: £2.6m) remaining outstanding as of 31 December 2023 (see Note 18 and
Note 27 for further details).

The Cast/income ratio (excluding one-off items) is 98% compared to 97% in prior year.

Impairment of intangible assets

As set out in the Company's accounting policies (see Note 1d), the Company undertakes an annual review of
impairment of all intangible assets held on the balance sheet to assess whether the fair value of each asset
is greater than the carrying value of the asset on the balance sheet. As a result of the fall in AUM during the
year, the Company assessed that the fair value of the intangible asset held in relation to NWB customer
relationships was less than the carrying value and therefore recorded an impairment charge of £2.5m (see
Note 20). In addition, the goodwill held on the balance sheet in relation to asset management businesses
acquired in the early 2000’s was also deemed to be impaired and a charge of £0.1m was taken to the income
statement (see Note 20).

Statement of financial position movements
The total assets held on the Balance Sheet of the Company increased by 2% to £1,530m (2022: £1,507m)
over the course of the year, mainly due to an increase in deposits from customers. Highlights are as follows:

e The customer loan book has reduced, with lending to customers decreased by 14% (2022: increase
14%) with £92m of net repayments taking the drawn book to £555m (2022: £647m). The loan to
customer deposit ratio ended the year at 41% (2022: 49%).

e Deposits from customers saw a 2% (2022: 36%) increase in the year to £1,354m (2022: £1,321m)
with an increase of 6% (2022: 24%) from Private and Investment Management Customers and 50%
(2022: 12%) growth mainly from the receipt of deposits from Company's relationship with Raisin, an
external deposit aggregation platform, as part of a strategic initiative to grow deposits and diversify
their source.

e The balance with the Bank of England has risen to £529m (2022: £458m) resulting from the decrease
in lending to customers (see Note 12).

« High quality debt securities held at amortised cost fell to £112m (2022: £117m), as the Company used
the increase in interest rates to enhance returns on assets whilst continuing to invest client's deposits
cautiously, through a combination of deposits with the Bank of England as set out above, bank term
deposits and loans and advances to banks via Certificates of Deposit, with £154m (2022: £116m)
held as at year end (see Note 13).

¢ Goodwill and intangibles have decreased to £28.2m (2022: £34.5m). This mainly reflects the
amortisation of customer relationships acquired in prior years, coupled with the additional impairment
