Stakeholder engagement and section 172 (1) statement
Section 172(1) of the Companies Act 2006 requires the Directors to act in a way that they consider in good
faith, would most likely promote the success of the Company for the benefit of its members as whole. In doing
this, Section 172(1) requires the Directors to have regard, amongst other matters, to:

(a) the likely consequences of any decision in the long-term; .

(b) the interests of the Company's employees;

(c) the need to foster the Company’s business relationships with suppliers, customers and others;

(d) the impact of the Company's operations on the community and the environment;

(e) the desirability of the Company maintaining a reputation for a high standard of business conduct; and

(f) the need to act fairly between members of the Company.

The Directors have acted in the way that they believe promotes the success of the Company for the benefit
of its stakeholders as a whole and maintaining a reputation for high standards of business conduct. In carrying
out this duty, the Directors have had regard for the matters set out in section 172(1)(a-f) of the Companies
Act 2006, as outlined above, in the decisions taken during the year ended 31 December 2023.

The Board’s key stakeholders, and a summary of how we engage with them, are set out below. Further
information is also provided in the Directors report on pages 10 to 27 of these financial statements.

Clients - Our clients are at the centre of everything we do, given our role as trusted adviser to them. We
communicate in many ways, meeting face to face, through quarterly valuations, by email, phone and post,
through our website and our client portal. Executive members of the Board meet with clients at organised
events, and we run client surveys and round tables periodically to learn what we do well and what we could
improve on,

Under the Consumer Duty, we ensure that the interests of our clients remain central to our culture and purpose
and are well embedded throughout our business. We hosted our first client council in 2023, which was driven
from our Consumer Duty survey, as part of our overall commitment to ensure we build our business around
client needs. At a Board level we have appointed the Chair of Board Risk and Compliance Committee to be
the Board champion for Consumer Duty.

Colleagues — It is critical that employees are connected directly to our strategy and aspirations as a Bank.
Management regularly engages with colleagues in a number of ways, including corporate inductions round
tables, regular business updates, townhalls, social events and monthly line manager calls.

in addition, the Company operates a Whistle Blowing policy which aims to increase staff awareness of being
able to speak out against bad practice within Brown Shipley or report potential wrangdoing without the fear of
any reprisals.

At Brown Shipley we operate a hybrid working pattern, allowing employees to flex their working location
between hame and office, with a minimum of three days in the office per week. The aim of our Remote Working
Policy is to provide flexibility to employees whilst continuing to foster the team culture of being in the office.

Our goal is to support employees to take ownership of their own development. Q-Lab is a central hub for all
Learning & Development activity. It contains resources for all colleagues to access which cover a variety of
topics, including Change Management, Digital, Finance, Leadership & Management, Resilience, and
Strategy. The resources range from short two minutes video clips to longer articles and podcasts. Hosting this
on Teams’ also supports communities and discussion groups.

Brown Shipley employees also took part in the Quintet Learning month which took place online in October
2023. There were over 50 sessions covering subjects including Growth Mindset, Coaching and Wellbeing, as
well as market insights using internal and external speakers, attended by over 400 colleagues across the
Group. In addition, we have launched a digital language learning platform in 2023, called Speexx. This
platform offers colleagues the opportunity to learn our core business languages (focused on English, French,
German and Dutch) at their own pace and on the go. Available via single sign on to any Quintet device, we
have seen over 200 learners across the Group start to access the platform to date.

The 2023 Graduate and Intern programmes launched in March. The Quintet graduate programme is a two-
year rotational experience mixed with projects, on the job learning and international exposure from across our
ocuSign Envelope ID: 20D25163-16F6-447B-B2B2-C72D09F8AB57 .

Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023
STRATEGIC REPORT

business and functions. The Internship programme immerses individuals in one area of the business for the
duration of the 8-week programme. Six interns and three graduates joined the Brown Shipley schemes in
2023.

We are committed to ensuring we attract, develop and engage a diverse employee base. We are signatories
of the Women in Finance Charter and have an agreed a target of 40% of women in senior leadership positions
by December 2024.

Brown Shipley achieved 35% of Senior Female Leaders in 2023, this was against the stretch target of 40%.
Since joining the charter in 2018, we have increased this metric from 18% and diversity and inclusion remains
a key priority for our Executive team. Our female representation on our Executive Committee has risen 2%
since 2022 and we have achieved our 40% target within this population.

To achieve our 40% target we continue to develop our Diversity & Inclusion (‘D&l') strategy and have seen
some positive progress, in particular with our relationships with Wealthiher and LGBT Great. During 2023,
we delivered ‘round table’ events sharing female leader career journey experiences of their diverse
‘characteristics’ in the workplace. We have also reviewed recruitment adverts/material to ensure gender
neutral language is used. These actions form part of our wider cultural strategy and deliverables regularly
communicated to the Executive Committee and the Board.

We have made positive progress and recognise that this needs to be sustained and embedded across our
population with a continued focus on ensuring that at all stages of the employee life cycle there is a policy of
gender neutrality and that there is gender balance in recruitment shortlists.

Our work with Wealthiher and our continued partnership with LGBT Great demonstrates our commitment to
broader diversity as part of our culture strategy. The Brown Shipley Executive Committee are committed to
continuing to build a diverse and inclusive culture where all employees are equally included and where all
employees are celebrated for their diversity.

Regulators —- The Company is a dual-regulated business with the Prudential Regulation Authority (‘PRA’) as
its lead regulator. The Chief Executive Officer (‘CEO’), Chief Financial Officer (‘CFO’) and Chief Risk Officer
(‘CRO’) maintain a regular and open dialogue with the PRA supervisory team through face to face and virtual
meetings, both scheduled and ad-hoc, and also via e-mail. There has been regular contact in the last 12
months on key risks to the business and clients and on the management actions taken.

The Financial Conduct Authority (‘FCA’) is the Company's conduct regulator. As the secondary regulator our
contact is more limited and Brown Shipley’s interaction is managed via the FCA's call centre supervision
model. In the last 12 months our interaction has taken the form of written responses to general industry wide
correspondence from the FCA.

The FCA introduced a new Consumer Principle that requires firms to act to deliver good outcomes for retail
customers under the Consumer Duty Regulation.

Shareholder ~ As a privately and wholly owned subsidiary of Quintet, the Company has only one shareholder
which we closely collaborate with. Currently two of Quintet’s Executive Committee members sit on our Board
and all Executive Committee members have a reporting line into their Head of Function at Quintet, in addition
to their reporting line within Brown Shipley. The current year has seen a continuing focus on leveraging Quintet
Group resources to enhance the capabilities provided by Brawn Shipley’s own support functions, as well as
identifying and implementing process efficiencies in the way those services are delivered to improve the
profitability of the Company and the Group.

Suppliers ~ Our suppliers are essential to ensuring the business runs smoothly and delivers a quality service
to its clients. We aim to ensure we pay all suppliers promptly and deal with them fairly. Management have
regular contact with our largest suppliers to ensure the relationships continue to run smoothly and are
committed to paying all suppliers in line with agreed terms.

Environment — We are committed to responsible carbon management and energy efficiency throughout our
organisation. We recognise that climate change is one of the most serious environmental challenges currently
threatening the global community and we understand we have a role to play in reducing greenhouse gas
emissions.
