Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023

DIRECTORS’ REPORT

The Directors present their Annual Report and financial statements of Brown Shipley for the year ended 31
December 2023.

Results and dividends

The loss for the year after taxation was £612k compared to a profit after taxation of £1.1m for the previous
year. No dividend has been declared or paid in respect of the current or previous year and the retained loss
has been transferred to reserves.

Composition of the Board
The Directors at the date of this Report are listed on page 2.

Chris Allen, Group CEO of Quintet, was appointed as a Director on 8 June 2023.

Andrew Curran, who has been CFO for over 10 years and with the Company for the past 23 years is retiring
from his role as a Director and CFO on 28 March 2024.The Board would like to thank him for his invaluable
contribution to Brown Shipley and the Quintet Group and wish him all the best for his retirement.

Directors’ Shareholdings
None of the Directors serving during or at the year-end (including their families and trusts) had any interests
requiring disclosure in the shares of the Company, the ultimate parent company or other Group companies.

Companies Miscellaneous Reporting Regulations 2018

The Directors are required to foster the Company’s business relationships with employees, suppliers,
customers and others under the Companies Miscellaneous Reporting Regulations 2018. Please refer to the
Strategic Report (pages 7 - 9) for this disclosure.

Gender Pay Gap

In April 2024, Brown Shipley will publish its Gender Pay Gap for 2023 on the Company’s website. The report
will highlight a continuing gap which in common with much of the financial services industry, reflecting the fact
that there are a greater number of men than women in more senior and more highly paid roles in the Company.

The Board is actively seeking to reduce the gap, though it recognises that this may take time. The Company
has enhanced its policies and benefits to encourage women to remain in the Company and continues to invest
in systems that supports flexible and remote working. The Board is also keen that the focus on gender does
not detract from the importance of encouraging wider diversity, and the Company’s policies and procedures
are reviewed regularly to reflect this.

Equal Opportunities Employer

Brown Shipley is an equal opportunities employer. We make all reasonable accommodations, considering
any physical or neurological conditions that may affect an individual's ability to carry out interviews or formal
assessments, and affording additional time or adaptation in approach, where required. This is intended to
ensure their application is not disadvantaged or prejudiced relative to any other applicant. This is mirrored in
our approach to providing full access to training and development opportunities on a universal basis,
irrespective of any disability.

We ensure that any colleague who may become disabled whilst employed, will be supported via appropriate
adjustments to working pattern, location, resources, remote working or role scope, in accordance with Brown
Shipley’s approach of valuing all colleagues equally, and supporting them to contribute fully to the success of
the business.

Remuneration Policy

Brown Shipley is committed to responsible and client centric remuneration practices which are reviewed and
assessed by the Board Remuneration and Nominations Committee (‘BRNC’) on an annual basis. The need
to reward Brown Shipley’s employees fairly and competitively based on performance is balanced with the
requirement to do so within the context of principled behaviour and actions. The Remuneration policy is
determined by the BRNC and aims to encourage the right behaviours and to reduce the risk that Brown
Shipley’s remuneration and incentive practices may give rise to conflicts of interest with its clients. These
conflicts of interests could originate from having remuneration, sales targets or other arrangements that could

