Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023
DIRECTORS’ REPORT

translate into an incentive to an employee to recommend preducts or services that are not appropriate to a
client's needs.

At 31 December 2023, the Committee members comprise of the Chair, the two independent Non-Executive
Directors and the Shareholder representatives who are all non-executive members of the Board of Directors.
The Committee is chaired by Tim Gillbanks. Committee meetings are held at least four times a year in line
with the Board committee cycle.

The BRNC assist and provide recommendations to the Board of Directors in its duties, to set guidelines on
compensation and benefits, to approve certain compensation and to assess the executive performance. This
is done by complying with the provisions of the FCA guidelines on compensation, as well as the group policies
and standards in setting the remuneration schemes of the Company.

The BRNC Oversees that:
e Remuneration for employees in Risk and Compliance is determined independently of other business

areas;
« No member of senior management is involved in deciding their own remuneration,
Approves

e The Remuneration Policy as well as individual nominations, remuneration packages and evaluation
for the most critical roles;

¢ Compensation for the Brown Shipley CEO;

« Overall changes to compensation plans;

« Compensation for Material Risk Takers (‘MRT’s) and Senior Management Function holders (‘SMF’s)
and Brown Shipley Executive Committee members; and

e Variable incentive compensation pools for Brown Shipley.

The BRNC's remuneration responsibilities are as follows:

1. Monitor the application of remuneration to ensure that policies and principles are being consistently
and effectively applied, seeking support and input from HR and Risk as appropriate. Liaise as required
with the other Board Specialised Committees and with the Risk function in relation to risk-adjusted
performance measures.

2. Approve the contents of the annual Remuneration Policy Statement for Pillar 3 external disclosure
(under Annex XII, part 2 Directive 2006/48/EC, point 15 added under 2010/76/EU). Ensure that all
provisions regarding disclosure of remuneration are fulfilled.

3. Meet all regulatory principles relating to remuneration including those set out within the UK Corporate
Governance Code.

4. Have due consideration to the FCA’s Conduct Rules and customer outcomes.

5. To agree remuneration levels (including bonuses, incentive payments and share awards) for
Executive Board Directors, Executive Committee members, Front Office senior managers, senior
Audit, Compliance and Risk staff. Ensure no Director or manager is involved in any decisions as to
their own remuneration.

6. To ensure that contractual terms on termination, and any payments made, are fair to the individual
and the Company, that failure is not rewarded and that the duty to mitigate loss is fully recognised.

7. Review the Remuneration Policy at least annually to ensure full regulatory compliance.

The Company falls into Tier 3 under the FCA Remuneration Code.

Composition of remuneration

Total compensation is comprised of fixed and variable compensation, and the ratio of fixed to variable
compensation depends on the position, role and responsibilities within Brown Shipley and varies from
employee to employee. In the UK, in accordance with the PRA implementation of the Capital Requirements
Directive V (‘CRDV’), variable compensation may be subject to ‘bonus caps’, which mandate a maximum ratio
of variable pay versus fixed compensation (1:1). Subject to shareholder approval, the applicable bonus cap
threshold can be increased (2:1).

