During December 2023 the Directors undertook an impairment review of the Company’s intangible assets and
goodwill. The impairment review compared the Carrying Value of these assets to the Recoverable Amount
(determined as the higher of the Fair Value Less Costs of Disposal and the Value in Use) for all Intangible
assets, except for N W Brown & Co (‘NWB’).

For the customer relationship intangible asset acquired with the purchase of NWB, the impairment review was
undertaken, using actual AUM multiplied by the AUM multiplier of 3% (2022: 3%) to assess fair value less
costs of disposal. The variation in method chosen was because the intangible was acquired in October 2019
and the business remains largely separable, the discounted cash flows (DCF) method was not considered
appropriate as the AUM is expected to generate cash flows beyond 15 years.

As a result of this review, the carrying value of intangible assets relating to customer relationships acquired
with the acquisition of NWB in 2019 were written down by £2,469k (2022: £2,355k) (see Note 20). Similarly,
goodwill relating to investment management businesses acquired in 2002 and 2003 were written down by
£45k (2022: £77k) (See Note 20).

Expected Credit losses (ECLs) increased by £465k during the year for financial assets on the statement of
financial position, all of which were individually assessed allowances (2022 increase of £106k).

