Goodwill

All Goodwill originates in the UK and relates to investment management businesses acquired in 2002 and
2003. The Goodwill is held at cost, less any impairment and historical amortisation charged (prior to adoption

of IFRS) and is tested for impairment on an annual basis.

An impairment review was carried out as at 31 December 2023 and an impairment of £45k was made as a
result (2022: £77k). The impairment reflects a fall in value of the underlying AUM acquired. In identifying the
recoverable amount or value in use, a discounted cash flow valuation was undertaken by the Company to

assess value in use with the following key assumptions:

Net cash flows have been projected over a 5-year period based on prior experience.

A discount rate of 8.19% (2022: 8%) has been applied to the net cash flows. The discount rate used
in 2023 is based on and similar to historic rates previously determined by us in conjunction with
Quintet (which were based on the Capital Asset rate of Return Method specific to the UK).
Management considers this rate appropriate given the sensitivity analysis conducted around discount
rates.

Income has been calculated at the current run rate for the two businesses acquired.

It is assumed that AUM will fall by 8% per annum (2022: 4%), determined by historical rates of decline.
Sensitivity analysis was performed on the growth rate and discount rates used in the reviews. The
impairment is most sensitive to a fall in AUM and hence income. For example, if the Company were
to assume that AUM fell by 20% p.a. the impairment would be an additional £115k. By contrast, if the

discount rate was 12% rather than 8.19%, the goodwill would only need to be impaired by a further
£37k.

57
ocuSign Envelope ID: 20025163-16F6-447B-B2B2-C72D09F8AB57 :

Brown Shipley & Co. Limited Annual Report 2023
NOTES TO THE ACCOUNTS FOR THE YEAR ENDED 31 DECEMBER 2023

Intangible assets — Customer relationships

The Company holds customer relationship intangible assets relating to a number of investment management
businesses acquired between 2008 and 2019. The assets are held at cost less accumulated amortisation and
accumulated impairment losses with the intangible assets amortised over ten to fifteen years depending on
their nature. The assets are tested for impairment on an annual basis.

An impairment review of the remaining customer relationship intangible assets was carried out as at 31
December 2023. The impairment review compared the Carrying Value of these assets to the Recoverable
Amount (determined as the higher of the Fair Value Less Costs of Disposal and the Value in Use). For all
assets other than the intangible asset held in relation to the customer relationships acquired with the purchase
of NWB (see below), a discounted cash flow valuation was undertaken by the Company to assess value in
use of these assets. The valuation resulted in a recoverable value of £17,941k as compared to a carrying
value of £10,467k, and therefore no requirement to impair the cumulative asset value. The analysis was based
on the following key assumptions:

« Net cash flows have been projected over a 5 year period or the remaining useful economic life
whichever is longer.

* Adiscount rate of 8.19% (2022: 8%) has been applied to the net cash flows. The discount rate used
in 2023 is based on and similar to historic rates previously determined by us in conjunction with
Quintet (which were based on the Capital Asset rate of Return Method specific to the UK).
Management considers this rate appropriate given the sensitivity analysis conducted around discount
rates.

e Income has been calculated at the current run rate for each business acquired.

« = Itis assumed that AUM will fall by between 0% and 10% per annum (2022: 0 - 10%), determined by
historical rates of decline for each business and excluding the one-off market fall of 12% in 2022.

*« Sensitivity analysis was performed on the growth rate and discount rates used in the reviews. The
impairment is most sensitive to a fall in AUM and hence income. The current excess of value in use
of customer relationship intangible assets assessed via the value in use method is £7,474k, if the
Company were to assume that AUM fell by 20% p.a. this excess would reduce by £4,069k resulting
in an additional impairment of £3,178k. By contrast, if the discount rate was 12% rather than 8%, this
this would result in additional impairment of £734k.

For the customer relationship intangible asset acquired with the purchase of NWB, the impairment review was
undertaken using actual AUM multiplied by an AUM multiple of 3% (2022: 3%) to assess fair value less costs
of disposal. The variation in method chosen was due to the fact that the intangible was acquired in October
2019 and the business still remains largely separable, the discounted cash flows (DCF) method was not
considered appropriate as the AUM is expected to generate cash flows beyond 15 years.

As a result of the review, it was determined that the recoverable value was £17,410k (2022: £21,726k) as
compared to a carrying value of £19,879k (2022: £24,081k) and therefore the carrying value of the NWB
intangible should has been written down by £2,469k (2022: £2,355k) to equal the recoverable value due to a
fall in AUM.

Sensitivity analysis was performed on the AUM valuation and the AUM multiple used in the reviews. The
impairment is equally as sensitive to a fall in the AUM multiple or a fall in AUM and hence income. For example,
if the Company were to assume that AUM fell by 20% p.a. the impairment would be an additional £3,347k. If
the multiple was 2.4% rather than 3.0%, the intangible would need to be impaired by a further £3,482k.

All intangible assets are considered as Level 3 for fair value assessment (i.e. they all have inputs which have
a significant effect on the fair value that are not based on observable data).

